# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reasoning Budget Arena — line-ending contract
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This repository is validated byte-for-byte. The answer datasets, the frozen
# question files, the score CSVs and the locked scorebooks are read, hashed and
# compared by scripts/ (validate_public_release_preview.py,
# build_*_answer_dataset.py, test_public_scanner.py). Locked-artifact digests
# are also quoted in the documentation.
#
# On Windows, git's default `core.autocrlf=true` rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. That changes the bytes on disk without changing the committed blob,
# so the same tree can pass validation on one machine and fail on another
# (observed: `withheld A/B` and `reviewed-FP` checks, and the extension answer
# dataset `--verify-only` runs).
#
# Contract: every text artifact is stored AND checked out with LF on every
# platform, so the working tree always matches the committed bytes. Binary
# assets are declared explicitly so they are never normalised.
#
# This rule set is deliberately two lines of behaviour: all files are text with
# LF unless declared binary. It applies to future files automatically and it
# required no renormalisation of any existing blob (the index already stores LF
# for every tracked text file).

# Text: normalise to LF in the repository and check out with LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.gguf  binary
*.bin   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
